The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```java
defaultClient.getConnectionManager().shutdown();
loadbalancer.shutdown();
```
Reason:
The `afterClass` method attempts to call `shutdown()` on `defaultClient` and `loadbalancer`, which can be null if they have not been initialized or have been set to null. This results in a NullPointerException (NPE) when the method tries to access methods on these null objects.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
if(defaultClient != null) {
    defaultClient.getConnectionManager().shutdown();
}
if(loadbalancer != null) {
    loadbalancer.shutdown();
}
```
Reason:
The patch adds null checks before calling `shutdown()` on `defaultClient` and `loadbalancer`. This prevents the NPE by ensuring that the shutdown methods are only called if the objects are not null, thus allowing the `afterClass` method to execute without throwing an exception.

